\chapter{Gradus ad Parnassum}

\begin{figure}[H]
\label{ly:cf_short}
\centering
\begin{lilypond}
	\language "english"
	{ d'1 e' fs' g' a' d' e' d' \bar "|." }
\end{lilypond}
\end{figure}

\noindent My first encounter with composition in the undergraduate curriculum
came in the form of species counterpoint. The exercises were straightforward:
given a \textit{cantus firmus} of eight or so measures, students would write a
melody that followed a very specific set of rules. It was often a mechanical
activity---only a handful of notes were ever possible at each juncture.

Still, I found that I had an advantage over my peers. As a pianist, I had spent
years playing polyphonic music in a way that the vocalists and other
instrumentalists had not. Unlike them, I rarely thought about the rules when
completing these exercises. It seems that, by studying piano, I had unwittingly
developed an intuition for counterpoint.

It's easy for computers to generate species counterpoint: they are incredibly
powerful and, given a \textit{cantus firmus}, can exhaustively search the space
of possible melodies until they find one that satisfies all of the rules. But
can they develop this sense of ``intuition"? In this thesis, we will explore
what it means for a computer to understand music.

\section{From beats to bytes}

As we study music---learning about notes and rhythms, melody and harmony, and
how history and theory come together---we begin to understand it more clearly.
We hear our favorite songs in a new language, one of chords and voicings and
layers inaccessible to the ordinary listener. Music is transformed from a blurry
collection of sounds into a concrete set of ideas.

% We achieve this understanding like children learning their first language: first
% by associating concepts we hear with 
% [assigning labels, associating concepts we hear with language (whether through
% lead sheets and scores or solmization and the Guidonian hand), generalizing
% concepts and thinking more abstractly].

% [Sapir---Whorf hypothesis applies to music]. [Neo-Riemannian theory is based on
% efficient voice leading, a concept that predates it by centuries---see Chopin's
% Prelude Op. 28, No. 4---but its development was hampered by the rigid system of
% tonality in Western music]. This effect is compounded when working with computer
% programs

Computers don't go through this process. They understand music only as well as
we describe it: a program designed solely to read sheet music will never
understand the un-notated \textit{rubato} hidden beneath the surface. The system
we use to represent musical information will determine the limits of the
programs we write, so we must consider our choice carefully.

What is the most meaningful representation of a piece of music? Audio files seem
like a good starting point, as they capture music in the same way as our ears.
But audio recordings have some undesirable qualities. For example, two clips of
the same piece can sound dramatically different due to variations in tempo,
tuning, timbre, recording equipment, and even the environment. Additionally,
recordings capture all kinds of noise, so---unless the music is entirely
synthesized---no two performances will produce the same audio file.

Perhaps scores are better. For one, they don't have noise. They also capture
more information about the music rather than a particular performance. But
scores emphasize unimportant notational aspects---consider the many aesthetic
and practical considerations that go into good engraving. And Western musical
notation comes in many loosely standardized flavors, making it difficult for
computers to parse.

MIDI\footcite{midi} appears to be a sort of middle ground: the format contains
much of the musical information of scores without any of the notational quirks,
and it was designed with computers in mind. Still, it's quite complex, encoding
all kinds of data that is more important to synthesizers than to musicians.

Lead sheets are promising: they lack the extraneous information found in scores
and audio files, and they excel at preserving the identity of a song even as we
change features like the accompaniment style or performing forces. However, this
model is too restrictive, requiring music to be formulated in terms of a melody
and chords.

Clearly, different styles of music lend themselves to different representations.
We need a general but meaningful way of representing various kinds of music that
balances several considerations. Some desirable properties for such a format
include the ease of parsing and generating music, high information density, and
a lack of extraneous information.

I propose a simple scheme for representing monophonic music. A voice is
represented as a sequence of events, either notes or rests, each with an
associated duration. Notes contain pitch information (letter name, accidental,
and octave number); durations are represented as fractions of a whole note.

\begin{minted}{haskell}
type Voice = [(Maybe Note, Duration)]

type Duration = Rational

data Note = Note Letter Accidental Octave

data Letter = A | B | C | D | E | F | G
data Accidental = DoubleFlat | Flat | Natural | Sharp | DoubleSharp
type Octave = Integer
\end{minted}

This representation has several limitations: it enforces a dodecaphonic
conception of pitch, requires durations to be defined relative to a
\textit{tactus}, encodes only monophonic voices, and neglects all aspects of
music beyond pitch and rhythm. Nevertheless, its simplicity makes this system
easy to work with for humans and computers alike, and it's still powerful enough
to represent virtually any monophonic line in the twelve-tone system.

\section{Case study: Species counterpoint}

Although this system is inherently monophonic, it can encode species counterpoint
quite easily: counterpoint is merely the juxtaposition of two monophonic voices.

We can model each rule of species counterpoint as a function that takes a piece
of (possibly unfinished) counterpoint as input and returns whether it follows
the rule. For example, we can define a function to check the range of our
melody:

\begin{minted}{haskell}
maxRangeRule music = maximum pitches - minimum pitches <= majorTenth
  where
    majorTenth = 16
    pitches    = map pitch (cp music)
\end{minted}

Now, we can write an algorithm to generate first-species counterpoint as
follows:

\begin{figure}[H]
	\small
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item Let $N$ be the set of all possible notes.
		\item Let $R$ be the set of rules for counterpoint.
		\item Let $\mathit{cf}$ be the sequence of notes in the given cantus firmus.
		\item
			Let $\mathit{cp}$ be the (initially empty) sequence of notes in our
			composed melody.
		\item \label{alg:branch}
			If $\mathit{cp}$ is finished (has the same number of notes as
			$\mathit{cf}$), then go to step~\ref{alg:done}. Otherwise:
			\begin{enumerate}
				\item \label{alg:for} For each note $n$ in $N$:
					\begin{enumerate}
						\item Append $n$ to $\mathit{cp}$.
						\item
							If $\mathit{cp}$ violates any of the rules in $R$,
							remove $n$ from $\mathit{cp}$ and return to
							step~\ref{alg:for}.
						\item Go to step~\ref{alg:branch}.
					\end{enumerate}
				\item
					No note satisfies the rules, so we need to backtrack. If
					$\mathit{cp}$ is empty, give up. Otherwise, remove the last note
					from $\mathit{cp}$ and return to step~\ref{alg:for}.
			\end{enumerate}
		\item \label{alg:done} Output $\mathit{cp}$.
	\end{enumerate}
	\hrule
	\caption{A backtracking algorithm for generating species counterpoint.}
	% \label{ly:basic_counterpoint}
\end{figure}

% TODO: cite rules
Let's see what the computer does with the \textit{cantus firmus} from
page~\pageref{ly:cf_short} and a basic set of rules found in [Fux] (see
\ref{app:basic_rules} for a complete list):

\begin{figure}[H]
	\begin{lilypond}
		\language "english"
		<<
		{ d'1 g' d' d'' fs' d' cs' d' }
		{ d'1 e' fs' g' a' d' e' d' \bar "|." }
		>>
	\end{lilypond}
	\caption{First-species counterpoint generated above a \textit{cantus firmus} with basic Fuxian rules}
	\label{ly:basic_counterpoint}
\end{figure}

The generated melody shows some promise: its contour is different from that of
the \textit{cantus} and there is a good balance of perfect and imperfect
consonances. However, it is quite disjunct, with no stepwise motion until the
final three notes, and there is voice crossing throughout. While these are not
issues addressed directly by Fux, they were highlighted in my undergraduate
education. Let's add some more rules commonly found in counterpoint texts (see
\ref{app:common_rules} for a complete list):

\begin{figure}[H]
\begin{lilypond}
	\language "english"
	<<
	{ d''1 g'' d'' e'' fs'' b' cs'' d'' }
	{ d'1 e' fs' g' a' d' e' d' \bar "|." }
	>>
\end{lilypond}
	\caption{First-species counterpoint generated above a \textit{cantus firmus}
	with a set of common rules}
\end{figure}

While this fixes the issues with voice crossing and very large leaps, it doesn't
seem to improve the musicality---in fact, the generated melody no longer has a
distinctive contour and there are now five consecutive sixths in the
counterpoint. Perhaps we can do better by adding ``rules" that encourage good
melodic writing (the complete list of melodic guidelines can be found in
\ref{app:guidelines}).

\begin{figure}[H]
\begin{lilypond}
	\language "english"
	<<
	{ d''1 g'' fs'' d'' cs'' d'' cs'' d'' }
	{ d'1 e' fs' g' a' d' e' d' \bar "|." }
	>>
\end{lilypond}
	\caption{Counterpoint generated with a set of rules to encourage good
	melodic writing}
\end{figure}

\begin{itemize}
	\item acceptable contrapuntal writing (would get an A)
	\item ascending leap of a perfect fourth up to the second note is strange
		\begin{itemize}
			\item artifact of the order in which we iterate over $N$
			\item a human would almost certainly step down to the leading tone
				here
		\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\chapter{Giant Steps}

We now have a program that composes passable species counterpoint, following the
``rules" of Fuxian counterpoint and writing acceptable melodies. Still, it
eschews some fundamental principles of melodic construction, sometimes producing
unmusical results. In this example, there are leaps to perfect intervals where a
step would yield contrary motion and measures spent vacillating between
neighboring notes.

\begin{figure}[H]
	\begin{lilypond}[staffsize=16]
		\language "english"
		<<
		{ d''1 b' d'' e'' cs'' d'' b' d'' b' d'' b' e'' e'' d'' }
		{ d'1 e' fs' g' a' d' e' d' e' fs' g' e' cs' d' \bar "|." }
		>>
	\end{lilypond}
	\caption{A rather unmusical melody generated over a long \textit{cantus firmus}}
\end{figure}

This isn't a particularly surprising result. After all, this is a lazy kind of
composition: the computer takes the path of least resistance, choosing the first
note that follows all the rules with no regard for the quality of the melody.

How do we encourage the computer to generate a good melody? Instead of iterating
over possible notes in some arbitrary order, what if we tried the ones that were
most likely to result in a pleasing melody first?

\section{How to write a melody}

Penalize:
\begin{itemize}
	\item leaps (especially if leaps have occurred recently)
	\item consecutive leaps (especially large leaps)
	\item frequently occurring notes (based on how recently they occurred)
	\item non-chord tones
	\item chromatic pitches
	\item large leaps from chromatic pitches
\end{itemize}

Prioritize:
\begin{itemize}
	\item steps following a leap (especially in the opposite direction)
	\item smaller intervals
	\item chord tones
	\item diatonic pitches
	\item stepwise resolution of non-diatonic pitches
\end{itemize}

